{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "The reply came in September, when the beans had begun to fatten in their pods and the leaves at the bottom of the wigwam were turning yellow at the edges. It was a small parcel this time, wrapped in brown paper and tied with garden twine, and her mother left it on the kitchen table beside the notebook without a word. Inside was a letter folded into quarters and a tin that had once held peppermints. The letter was short. Her cousin wrote that the three beans had gone into a pot on the windowsill, because the wind off the sea was too strong for canes, and that two of them had come up. The second had flowered late, in August, but it had flowered. At the bottom, in the same careful writing, her cousin had added that she had started keeping a notebook too. The girl opened the tin. It was full of seeds she did not recognise: flat and papery, round and black, some striped like tiny shells. Each kind was folded into a twist of paper with a name pencilled on it. Marigold. Nasturtium. Sweet pea. Something she could not read. She sat at the table for a long time, turning the twists over in her fingers, while the kettle ticked as it cooled. Then she went outside with a basket and began to pick the fattest pods, splitting one with her thumbnail to check the beans inside were speckled and dry. She would save the best for next year. She would save some for Aberdeen."}
